{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## The mechanism is MEASURED, not inferred — and the three candidate fixes, argued\n\n### Correcting one point in the hand-off: the mechanism was tested directly\n\nThe reading \"thread-method timeout kills the worker\" is not an inference from the\nzero-`Failed: Timeout`-plus-`node down` pattern. It was run as a three-armed discriminating\nexperiment against a purpose-built 25 s-body probe test under `-n 2 --dist worksteal`, in a\nworktree at `6a5c88027c5`:\n\n    ARM A  ini as shipped (timeout=20, timeout_method=thread)\n           -> [gw0] node down: Not properly terminated\n              worker 'gw0' crashed while running '<probe>'\n              zero `Failed: Timeout`            == the CI signature, reproduced\n    ARM B  identical, but --timeout-method=signal\n           -> E  Failed: Timeout (>20.0s) from pytest-timeout.\n              1 failed, 1 passed in 20.87s      worker SURVIVES; clean verdict\n    ARM C  identical, but --timeout=300 --timeout-method=thread\n           -> 2 passed in 25.88s\n\nArm B changes ONLY the method and converts the crash into a verdict, so the method is what\nturns an expiry into a worker death. Arm C changes ONLY the budget and removes the failure, so\nthe budget is what makes it fire. The source agrees: `pytest_timeout.py:542` (`timeout_timer`,\nthe thread path) calls `os._exit(1)`; `pytest_timeout.py:502` (`timeout_sigalrm`, the signal\npath) calls `pytest.fail()`. A budget expiry inside an xdist worker therefore CANNOT print\n`Failed: Timeout` — the process is gone before pytest can report anything.\n\nSo the tension in the original report dissolves: zero `Failed: Timeout` is a *prediction* of\nthis mechanism, not evidence against it. Bug `albinic-agrologic-dolphin` independently proved\nthe same thing a fortnight ago and used `--timeout=20 --timeout-method=thread` as its\ndeliberate crash-INDUCING amplifier.\n\n### Independently, pristine main reproduces locally\n\n`pytest -m integration -n 4 --dist loadgroup -q -p no:cacheprovider --timeout=20\n--timeout-method=thread`, run on an untouched worktree at `origin/main`, produced\n`[gw3] node down: Not properly terminated` and `[gw1] node down: Not properly terminated`,\nwith zero `Failed: Timeout` — the reported integration symptom, on this machine, with no change\napplied. The same command at `--timeout=300` on the fixed tree: **zero** node-downs,\n174 passed in 182.61 s.\n\n### Why a higher ceiling, and not the two alternatives\n\n**Rejected — `timeout_method = \"signal\"`.** It would make overruns report honestly instead of\nkilling workers, which is superficially attractive. But `thread` is load-bearing for the reason\nbug `balsamy-eloquent-iggypops` adopted it: SIGALRM is delivered to the main thread and cannot\nfire while that thread is blocked in a C-level `flock`/socket/subprocess call — the exact hang\nshape this suite exercises, and the shape that produced the original 60-minute silent CI cell.\nThat ticket proved it with a blocking-`flock` probe: unguarded it hung until SIGKILL; under\n`--timeout=3 --timeout-method=thread` it aborted in 3.36 s and NAMED the test. Switching to\n`signal` buys prettier reporting for CPU-bound overruns at the cost of reopening the real hang\nclass, and `test_ci_workflow_parity.py::test_shared_pytest_suite_has_a_per_test_hang_guard`\npins `--timeout-method=thread` on every lane precisely so this is not quietly undone.\n\n**Rejected — per-test `@pytest.mark.timeout` overrides on the whole-tree scanners.** The\ncrashing set DRIFTS: the two failing jobs differ by a member (`test_reconciler_jira_seam_clean_\nheldout` in one, `test_residual_drain_seam_clean_heldout` in the other). What is stable is a\nPOPULATION of whole-tree scanners sitting in an 18.71-29.93 s band against a 20 s line, not a\nlist of eleven. Marking today's eleven leaves tomorrow's twelfth unmarked, so the fix would\nhave to be re-applied after every scan-cost drift — and each marker is a permanent silent\nexemption from the guard on a test that never needed one. It also inverts the instrument:\na per-test hang budget exists to bound the suite, so it should be sized to the suite rather\nthan the suite annotated to fit the budget. Worth noting the shape is already visible — the\nonly >20 s entry surviving in a failing run is `22.01s test_a_marked_test_may_outrun_the_ini_\nbudget`, which passes solely because it carries an override.\n\n**Chosen — restore the ceiling to 300 s.** It is the value `balsamy-eloquent-iggypops`\nestablished, sized explicitly to DWARF the slowest legitimate test rather than to sit above it;\nit is what eleven other `--timeout=` lanes in this repo already use; and it needs no per-test\nannotation, so it survives scan-cost drift. Against the measured 29.93 s worst case it carries\n~10x headroom, which is the property that failed here: 20 s sat INSIDE the band of legitimate\ncosts, so ordinary variance — and then the 11 worker restarts re-paying coverage startup, which\npushed survivors from ~16 s to 19.0-19.95 s — decided which innocent tests died.\n\nStory `bold-abeyant-indri`'s genuine improvement is kept intact: the budget still lives in\n`[tool.pytest.ini_options]`, so `make test` and a bare local `pytest` carry it, which they did\nnot before. `timeout_method` and `timeout_func_only` are untouched.\n\n### What the new test adds over pinning the number\n\n`test_the_configured_budget_is_the_measured_one` asserts the budget EQUALS whatever is written\ndown; it cannot distinguish a measured value from a wrong one, and it passed throughout this\nincident. `tests/unit/test_timeout_budget_headroom.py` asserts the invariant instead — the\nbudget must clear the measured worst legitimate body by 2x (>= 59.86 s), and must still be a\nreal guard (<= 600 s, `thread` method). It fails at 20 with `assert 20.0 >= 59.86` and passes\nat 300; re-introducing `timeout = 20` returns it to red."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787420117611958001,"uuid":"967a157b-2903-4c96-be4e-92e27142013e"}